omarchy-deck
============

The Steam Deck enablement layer for Omarchy, shipped as a pacman package.

Installed at /usr/share/omarchy-deck/README on every target installed from the
Omarchy Deck ISO. If you are reading this file on a running system, the seam-S4
pipeline worked end to end.


What this package is
--------------------

Omarchy installs itself as a pacman package, so everything the Steam Deck needs
at install time ships the same way rather than as edits to the ISO builder.
That is seam S4 of the fork plan (docs/tasks/T5-fork-plan.md §3), and the line
it draws is:

    patches to the ISO builder  ->  BUILD-time behaviour only
    this package                ->  everything INSTALL-time

The line is not tidiness. Phase 4 of the roadmap wants the Deck layer to be
portable to an already-installed Omarchy system (docs/tasks/T7-enablement-layer
.md); anything that lives as a patch to somebody else's builder cannot come
along.


How it gets here
----------------

  1. builder/build-iso.sh (patched by iso/overlay/patches/deck-packages.patch)
     builds this recipe with makepkg, straight into the ISO's offline mirror.
  2. It is stripped from the online `pacman -Syw` target list -- it exists in
     no online repository, and leaving it in aborts a ~6 GB download.
  3. It is added back to prune-offline-mirror.sh's keep-set, or the prune
     deletes it again: the online transaction never selected it.
  4. It is excluded from deck-nvidia-dry-run.sh's ONLINE resolve, for the same
     reason as (2) -- while that guard still asserts the exclusion was
     deliberate and that the name really is in deck-install.packages.
  5. deck-install.packages carries the name, so pacstrap installs it on every
     target from the offline mirror.

All five read one declaration in the patched builder, derived from the
pkgbuilds/ directory itself. A desync between any two of them is silent until
pacstrap fails on the target.


What is NOT here yet
--------------------

This is a skeleton: it proves the pipeline, it carries no behaviour. Landing
inside it later:

  T5e   boot-to-Gaming-Mode, the encryption default, the lock producers
  T5f   the rotation surfaces, and the ALPM hook that re-applies them
  T12   omarchy-deck-apply-patches, registered as its own configure_deck step

Until those land, the only thing this package asserts about a machine is that
the ISO that installed it was built by the Omarchy Deck overlay.
